{"about":"0f7a8b30a80188d2b98cfe5cccc160f1b2c70b259fbc2ab374c714f493c4a6bd","body":"Despite Popper's explicit denials, the act of preferring a well-corroborated theory for practical action implicitly assumes that past success is a reliable indicator of future performance. If corroboration reports only past survival and offers no logical bridge to future reliability, then preferring Theory A over Theory B for the purpose of navigation, engineering, or medicine is irrational unless one assumes the uniformity of nature—the very principle of induction Popper rejected. To say 'this theory worked before, so I will use it now' is functionally identical to inductive reasoning. The cost of admitting this is the collapse of Popper's claim to have solved the problem of induction; his system would then rest on a pragmatic form of induction he ostensibly outlawed.","kind":"mini.commitment-proposal.v1","named":"The preference for better-corroborated theories inevitably smuggles induction back into Popper's framework."}